[Music]  over the past 25 years Shawn Brock has  risen to the top of the culinary world  he's the founding chef of the restaurant  husk in Charleston South Carolina named  the best new restaurant by Bon Appetit  in 2011 HUS now has four locations  across the south brach has one two James  Beard Awards released two best-selling  cookbooks and is featured on the Netflix  series chefs table this year at the age  of 41 he walked away from it all we met  up with him at his home in Nashville I  was under the illusion that I was you  know unbreakable at his peak chef and  southern food evangelist Sean Brock was  operating eight restaurants in five  cities including the acclaimed husk it's  so fast-paced and it's so stressful that  you'd lose access to the part of your  brain where all the rational  decision-making occurs and so you just  keep making mistakes over and over and  it's easy to get trapped in that tornado  and you were trapped in it I was in the  center of that tornado I was conducting  it Brock admits he was a workaholic it  strikes me that you're you're pretty  obsessive about everything you do yeah  his body was shutting down in 2016 he  was diagnosed with a chronic autoimmune  disease that can be aggravated by stress  my immune system started attacking  itself I woke up one day with with  double vision and you can't use a sharp  knife with double vision and you can't  juggle flaming hot pins and that was the  first time in my life where I kinda had  my hands tied he was frustrated angry  and drinking his friends and family were  alarmed especially his mom Renee were  you worried a few years ago absolutely  cuz his dad you know was a workaholic  and he dropped over from heart attack at  39 so  I held my breath until he reached 40 I  literally held my breath I turned there  was a period of time I didn't know him  he was just he was you didn't recognize  him  you look great you were a different  person telling different person weren't  you completely what had happened  alcohol yeah it's different close  friends intervened and Brock went to  rehab even when you know you have to  change sometimes it can be very hard to  change I think that's why the universe  didn't give me a choice do you like  yourself better now yeah absolutely  I've never been happier healthier or  better cook than I and than I am now how  does it change your cooking being  healthy and being happy is allowed me a  clarity that I didn't know existed  honestly this year he packed up his  knives you walked away from Husker which  is something you spent a lot of time  creating and when you a lot of accolades  it's something that a lot of people were  very confused about to build something  up and put so much time into it inferred  to be so successful and so important and  just to be able to walk away from that  was an incredible gift that I gave  myself Barak hasn't worked in a kitchen  in a year instead he got married and had  a son Leo I tried to buy all my food  here and visits the Nashville farmers  markets regularly that's a formidable  potato I'm gonna buy some because my son  Leo likes sweet potatoes the first time  I made food for Leo was pretty pretty  humbling pretty embarrassing why what  happened so I had this idea that I would  come here and pick out like 15 to 20  different varieties of squash or so you  can say this and line up all the spoons  for him to taste each one yeah he is the  gag  I gotta get these Peas  so this is my friend Thomas over here  he's the king of peas with his pea  machine taste that that vibrancy that's  the way the food has always tasted in  the south it's the food that I grew up  with over the years unfortunately a lot  of this flavor got bred out these Peas  inspired the chicken and dumplings  recipe in his new cookbook South  published this fall these are fancy  dumplings  cuz I can't make yours it's a  celebration of the unheralded  Appalachian cuisine Brock says runs  through his veins absorbed growing up in  his grandmother's garden in Virginia  coal country ever had leather britches  leather britches maybe men know about  sake beans sake beans today he has a  vegetable garden inked onto his arm  how long did it take you to get that put  on like 250 hours Wow really  this food will be the star at his  upcoming restaurant project in Nashville  where Brock will also try to transform  the restaurant business the way he's  done his own life I think it's time in  this industry it used to be your  decision making was based around what  you're capable of doing think how far  can you push it now it's more what can  we achieve while staying happy and  healthy the mental and physical wellness  of the team will be paramount you're  trying to change the culture effectively  well in the industry we bend over  backwards all day every day to take care  of the guests and in the mean time we're  breaking ourselves you've described this  as kind of the second act of your life  it really is this is the restaurant that  I'll serve my last meal in this is the  restaurant where I'll clock out for the  final time  that's quite a transformation his two  restaurant project named after his  grandmother Audrey opens next spring it  will have a shorter menu to alleviate  stress on staff a special lighting  system that will also be a soundproof  sanctuary room for staff to decompress  meditate or seek other distressing  Fairly I like how he's thinking yeah so  she  bring him on I can't wait to go the new  restaurant it's so interesting with him  because even though you realize he's  changed when you see what he did for his  son in terms of lining up to 15 spoons  bones you know he's still obsessive  about everything he does food look  really I went off the screen I want to  say I've never eaten beans like those  beans I love when his mom was rubbing  his back when she was talking to him  clearly it's very tender between that  was it was a tough period that they got  through

